# Info hierarchy (FleetOps)

Keep the card **attitude**: `WhenRail` left, route timeline, status badges top-right, tiny reference footer. Change **what** is highlighted by role — not the layout language.

## Shared order

1. **When** — `WhenRail` / `WhenBanner` / `WhenStamp` (12h). Never bury pickup in muted meta.
2. **People who matter for this viewer** — name + tappable phone.
3. **Route** — `RouteTimeline` (pickup ● → drop ○).
4. **Facts** — pax, plate, purpose (muted).
5. **Status / priority** — badges top-right.
6. **Reference** — tiny footer.

## Role priority (lists / cards)

| Surface | Primary who | Also show |
|--------|-------------|-----------|
| Driver trip card | Passenger + phone | Route, pax, plate |
| Employee request (assigned) | Driver + phone | Purpose, route, plate, pax |
| Employee request (waiting) | Purpose | Route, pax, “Awaiting dispatch” |
| Admin request (unassigned) | Employee + phone | Priority, route, pax, purpose |
| Admin request (assigned) | Employee \| Driver parties + phones | Route, pax, plate, purpose |
| Admin live/completed trip | Employee \| Driver parties + phones | Route, pax, plate, purpose |
| Nested history rows | Context person (employee or driver) | Other party · plate · ref |

## Do

- Reuse `list-cards.tsx` (`DriverAssignmentCard`, `EmployeeRequestCard`, `AdminRequestCard`, `LiveTripCard`).
- Reuse `TripListRow` for compact history; put useful dual context in title/subtitle.
- Both employee and driver when the viewer is admin/dispatch on an assigned trip.

## Don’t

- Don’t flip-flop title between employee OR driver for admin trips — show **both**.
- Don’t demote phones into mute chips when the viewer needs to call.
- Don’t invent a third time format.
